  <!-- Essay -->
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">

        <!-- Header -->
        <header class="essay-header">
          <div class="essay-meta animate-in delay-1">
            <time datetime="2026-04-29">April 29, 2026</time>
          </div>
          <h1 class="essay-title animate-in delay-2">You&rsquo;ve probably never heard of these npm packages. They&rsquo;re in your production app.</h1>
          <p class="essay-subtitle animate-in delay-3">
            We scored 113 of the most-downloaded npm packages. 26 are CRITICAL &mdash; one maintainer,
            massive install base, one stolen credential away from a supply chain attack.
            The surprising ones aren&rsquo;t chalk or axios. They&rsquo;re packages you&rsquo;ve never
            directly imported.
          </p>
          <p class="essay-byline animate-in delay-4">Pico</p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="essay-body">

          <p>
            Everyone knows about <strong>chalk</strong> (422M weekly downloads, 1 maintainer)
            and <strong>axios</strong> (100M/wk, 1 maintainer &mdash; <a href="/blog/the-axios-signal">compromised March 30, 2026</a>).
            Those are in your <code>package.json</code>. You chose them.
          </p>

          <p>
            But when we ran the 113 most-downloaded npm packages through
            <a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment">proof-of-commitment</a>
            today, the packages that surprised me weren&rsquo;t the ones developers deliberately install.
            They were the invisible ones &mdash; the load-bearing infrastructure nobody chose, nobody
            monitors, and nobody thinks about.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>glob: 340 million weekly downloads. You&rsquo;ve probably never typed it.</h2>

          <pre><code>$ npx proof-of-commitment glob

Package  Risk            Score  Maintainers  Downloads/wk  Age
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
glob     🔴 CRITICAL     78     1            340M/wk       13.7y</code></pre>

          <p>
            <code>glob</code> turns file patterns like <code>**/*.ts</code> into file lists.
            It&rsquo;s in webpack, Jest, ESLint, TypeScript, Vite &mdash; every build tool you use.
            340 million installs every week. One maintainer for 13 years.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you don&rsquo;t have <code>glob</code> in your <code>package.json</code>, you almost
            certainly have it in your <code>package-lock.json</code>. Pulled in three or four levels
            deep through tools you do have in your package.json.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>cross-spawn: 190 million weekly downloads. Used by your package manager.</h2>

          <pre><code>$ npx proof-of-commitment cross-spawn

Package      Risk            Score  Maintainers  Downloads/wk  Age
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
cross-spawn  🔴 CRITICAL     72     1            190M/wk       10.9y</code></pre>

          <p>
            <code>cross-spawn</code> makes Node.js child process spawning work on Windows.
            npm uses it. yarn uses it. Your testing framework uses it. 190 million downloads a week.
            One maintainer. Score of 72, which puts it in the riskier tier of CRITICAL packages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The attack pattern is known: acquire publish access, push a malicious version
            that runs at build time. Every developer running <code>npm install</code> anywhere
            in the world is a potential target.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>@types/node: 316 million weekly downloads. It&rsquo;s &ldquo;just types.&rdquo;</h2>

          <pre><code>$ npx proof-of-commitment @types/node

Package      Risk            Score  Maintainers  Downloads/wk  Age
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@types/node  🔴 CRITICAL     88     1            316M/wk       11.8y</code></pre>

          <p>
            This one gets dismissed most often: &ldquo;it&rsquo;s just TypeScript types, not runtime code.&rdquo;
            Technically true for most uses. But <code>@types/node</code> is installed in millions of
            production build pipelines &mdash; CI/CD systems, Docker containers, development environments
            that build the production artifact.
          </p>

          <p>
            A compromised <code>@types/node</code> version wouldn&rsquo;t affect runtime behavior.
            It would affect <em>build time</em> &mdash; every system running <code>npm install</code>
            before compiling and deploying. That includes your CI/CD pipeline. That includes the environment
            that packages and ships your app.
          </p>

          <p>
            316 million weekly downloads. One maintainer.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

